{"content":[{"c":["FREEDMEN*"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Members of a Jewish synagogue in Jerusalem (",{"href":"?bref=Acts.6.9","r":"Acts.6.9","rawR":"Acts.6.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.6.9","value":"Acts.6.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 6:9"},"), descended from Jews who had been captured and taken to Rome by the general Pompey (106–48 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"), then later released. Pompey found that the Jews adhered so strictly to their religious and national customs that they were worthless as slaves."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Not all the freedmen returned to Jerusalem; some stayed in Rome. In the time of the Roman writer Pliny, a freedman was described as a “mean commoner.” The freedmen (or “Freed Slaves,” ",{"c":["nlt"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},") derived their name from a Latin term for one manumitted, or the son of such a former slave. ",{"c":["See"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=Libertines_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"Libertines_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"Libertines_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Libertines"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Freedmen","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Acts 6:9","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Acts.6.9","rawQueryValue":"Acts.6.9","sourceRefExact":"Acts.6.9"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"372e715b59b41ba12fe9e6870ec29169d391115c0d962ff858bf25c361b3e3b6","sourceItemName":"Freedmen","sourceItemOrder":79,"sourceMember":"Articles/F.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Freedmen","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Freedmen*","title":"Freedmen*","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}